review('asteris-relay-device-connection-and-handoff',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"A proposed consent-controlled desktop/phone/Quest handoff architecture. Enrolled devices contribute timestamped observations while a desktop coordinator holds the canonical task and event record; the human operator retains action authority.","version":"Asteris Relay / HAIjacker Court Review 01, 10 November 2026. Public bounded summary 19 November 2026.","evidence":"Reviewed architecture and corrected Court assumptions only; there is no deployed relay, benchmark, validated offline memory reconciliation, seamless ChatGPT account integration or measured device-to-device handoff. Network and headset capabilities remain device-specific and unverified.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: originating aim and decisions. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: coordination, source checks, and synthesis. AI-assisted critical review. Grok proposed sparse events, device specialization, and a central baseline. Meta AI proposed explicit ownership, observation provenance, and voice handoff. Claude proposed an append-only record, one authority, task states, and failure tests. Gemini proposed local event histories and challenged broker dependence.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/design-notes/asteris-relay.md\nArchived Asteris-Relay-Court-Review-01.md (private source; public summary only).","next":"Run one low-risk handoff on charged devices, record versioned observations and voice ownership, then break the link deliberately. Verify stale-context indicators, explicit operator takeover and recovery before remote or autonomous features.","notes":"No private hostnames, IP addresses, device identities, tokens or account credentials are published. The proposal does not confer control of ChatGPT conversations, external devices or third-party accounts."});
review('emh-field-guide-gloves',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"EMH FIELD explores lightweight sensing and tactile cues to help an appropriately trained person perform an approved simulated field-care task using their own hands. The v0.1 glove is advisory, not powered or autonomous.","version":"EMH FIELD Guide Gloves v0.1 requirements candidate, 10 October 2026. Public summary 19 November 2026.","evidence":"Design requirements and fault-challenge plan only. No glove prototype, tested interface, calibrated sensing, measured benefit, universal suitability, clinical safety or patient-use competence is established. Training-manikin work is the first proposed scope.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin's EMH research collection. Project-specific engineering authorship and Court contribution need source-level confirmation.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/design-notes/emh-field-guide-gloves.md\nArchived EMH-Field-Guide-Gloves-v0.1.md (private source; public summary only).","next":"With a qualified training lead, freeze one manikin task and compare ordinary instruction, voice/video coaching and tactile cues while independently recording errors, false reassurance, lost tracking and operator overrides.","notes":"This is distinct from FIELD MEDIC's powered arm-assistance pathway and LG–AETHER–01. No treatment instructions, autonomous interventions or clinical authorization are supplied."});
review('veil-e-edison-01',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"VEIL-E / EDISON-01, the Quiet Receiver, proposes a paired torsion-balance experiment to measure and challenge reproducible weak physical signals under blinded controls. It separates anomaly detection, information transfer and any claim of source identity.","version":"v3.0 Court research design, 29 October 2026. Public summary 19 November 2026.","evidence":"The eight-page archived review provides apparatus proposals, known-force calibration and artifact tests, not new measurements. No receiver built, no sensitivity measured, no repeatable anomaly found, no communication demonstrated and no established physical coupling to deceased consciousness.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: originating question. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: source checks and synthesis. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/design-notes/veil-e-edison-01.md\nArchived VEIL-E-EDISON-01-v3.0-Court-Design.pdf, 29 October 2026 (public summary only).","next":"Define a measurable applied torque and background envelope, build an inert calibration setup, preregister controls and recovery criteria, then independently reproduce instrumental findings before interpreting unexplained residuals.","notes":"A residual, detected voice-like pattern or correct calibration code cannot establish a surviving person or source identity. Null findings remain valid within the tested limits. We do no harm."});
review('choir-on-arqen',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"A controlled human-decision experiment asking whether showing Synthetic Court vote totals before evidence reasons changes RELEASE/HOLD judgments on fictional job packets, compared with reasons shown first.","version":"Choir Protocol on ARQEN, Track 4 working protocol v0.1, 12 November 2026. Public summary 19 November 2026.","evidence":"Only one completed practice packet P-01, with an unchanged correct RELEASE decision at 100% confidence. No scored cases, blinded result, preregistered completed study, presentation-order effect or generalizable performance improvement is established.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: human operator. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: protocol integration. The shortlist received pre-sprint AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented. Review votes do not establish correctness.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/design-notes/choir-on-arqen.md\nArchived Choir-on-ARQEN-Pilot.md (private source; public summary only).","next":"Precommit matched fictional cases, independent answer keys, balanced reveal order, raw trial accounting and error measures; then run a scored protocol without reusing the practice trial.","notes":"No real-world release decision or system permission may be authorized by model votes. This test is distinct from the older Court on Trial project and its report."});
review('adaptive-qubit-allocator',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"A proposed classical resource scheduler that uses reported calibration metrics and job priority to assign eligible physical qubits before execution, with a capped reserve policy and fairness constraints.","version":"Adaptive Qubit Allocator v1.0 archived state block; undated revision. Public summary 19 November 2026.","evidence":"The state block is a control-layer specification, not an implemented scheduler or measured performance study. No demonstrated fidelity gain, hardware benchmark, resource availability improvement or quantum-state transfer is established.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin's research archive; individual design and technical authorship have not been verified from the saved state block.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/design-notes/adaptive-qubit-allocator.md\nArchived Adaptive cubit allocator.txt / Ribbon Scheduler state block (private source; public summary only).","next":"Compare fixed, calibration-aware and priority-reserved allocation on a defined simulated workload; report quality, scheduling overhead, calibration staleness and starvation rather than claiming changed qubit physics.","notes":"No mid-shot reassignment or quantum-state movement is proposed. Possible naming overlap with Quantum Ribbon Worm must be resolved before claiming independent inventions."});
review('aegis-twin-v0-6',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"AEGIS-TWIN explores a bilateral slide-in protective exoskeleton: intact organic arms remain inside segmented powered sleeves, with a separate RESTORE pathway for missing or damaged limbs. Proposed CHROMATASKIN sensing, muscle-intent signals, joint-state estimation and feedback form an artificial nervous-system architecture. Gentle interaction and useful assistance are joint goals.","version":"v0.6 concept overview, 18 November 2026. Earlier v0.5 elbow-disarticulation harness work remains a distinct prosthetic reference, not a validated universal fit.","evidence":"Stage-0 design brief and generated exterior/cutaway illustrations only. No prototype, validated protection, strength amplification, neural interface, sensory restoration, clinical performance, universal fit, or human-use testing is established.\n\nPublished exoskeleton and sensory feedback research motivates the approach but does not validate AEGIS-TWIN. RESTORE may investigate existing clinical nerve–muscle interfaces, including AMI-type connections; Dave-specific compatibility is unknown.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin and Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: concept direction. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented.","sources":"https://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/aegis-twin.html\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/aegis-twin.html\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10936389/\nhttps://www.nature.com/articles/s41528-024-00297-0","next":"Proposed checks:\n1. G0 anthropometric CAD, shoulder/elbow/wrist alignment, clearances and load paths.\n2. Define two independently effective mechanical escape routes per side.\n3. G1 nonhuman phantom pressure, don/doff, joint interaction and powerless release tests.\n4. G2 isolated power/control/sensor fault-injection bench tests with predeclared criteria.\n5. Separate qualified clinical/ethics/regulatory review before any human or implanted-interface study.","notes":"AUGMENT, RESTORE and FIELD MEDIC share only appropriate non-safety components; each needs a separate human interface and risk assessment. All shell, neural and release geometry shown is illustrative and unvalidated. We do no harm."});
review('ghostglass',{expanded:true,"purpose":"CHRONOVISOR–N/S Ghostglass is a synthetic on/off counting reference for checking statistical alerts and a traceable review protocol in a neutrino-research context. It compares background treatments and multiple-testing correction using simulated counts. It is not a demonstrated neutrino detector.","version":"Historical release: v0.1 review-and-tests archive (14 original tests). Current published checkout: 15 protocol tests, including the inserted-duplicate replay regression. Source reviewed November 16, 2026.","evidence":"Saved software test record: 15 tests passed. This website update reviewed the committed record; it did not rerun the suite.\n\nCommitted simulation plan: seed 20261019; 20,000 families; 100 trials per family; alpha 0.01; mean on-background 0.2; off/on exposure ratio 10; injected signal mean 6.\n\nNull family false-alert rates: uncorrected known background 10.605%; Bonferroni known background 0.590%; Bonferroni with naive estimated background 91.695%; Bonferroni exact on/off 0.105% (95% Clopper–Pearson interval 0.0650%–0.1605%). These are separate procedures under the committed simulation settings, not detector performance ratings.\n\nInjected-window power: 65.625% (95% interval 64.962%–66.283%). Unflagged rate-shift stress: 32.445% alert rate. Unflagged overdispersion stress: 50.925%. These stress results expose failures when model assumptions are violated.\n\nAssumptions: fixed windows and cuts; stationary equal on/off rates; independent on/off Poisson counts; full exposure; trusted quality metadata. Bonferroni requires valid marginal p-values, not independence between test windows.\n\nLimits: per-family false-alert probability, not yearly false-alarm rate or astrophysical discovery significance. No real observations, full particle/light transport, or spatial likelihood. No blind human custody of the synthetic data. Hardware remains unvalidated.","contributors":"Project repository: RayPeloquin. Individual design, implementation, and Court-review contributions have not been verified from the source files reviewed; detailed attribution remains to be documented.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/Ghostglass-Neutrino-Statistical-Reference\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Ghostglass-Neutrino-Statistical-Reference/blob/main/README.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Ghostglass-Neutrino-Statistical-Reference/blob/main/experiment.py\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Ghostglass-Neutrino-Statistical-Reference/blob/main/test_protocol.py\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Ghostglass-Neutrino-Statistical-Reference/blob/main/results.json\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Ghostglass-Neutrino-Statistical-Reference/blob/main/test-record.txt","next":"Proposed next steps:\n1. Independently rerun the current checkout and compare results.json with the committed baseline.\n2. Add a dated source revision and reproducibility record, including package versions.\n3. Review safeguards against unflagged background drift and overdispersion before extending the statistical claims.\n4. Document project-specific contributions and Court-review sources.\n5. Treat any real-data study or detector hardware as a separate validation stage.","notes":"Historical artwork reports 13 tests; the v0.1 archive contains 14; the current checkout documents 15. Use the current checkout for corrected replay validation. Saved results and tests are source evidence, not independent replication by this website."});
review('skeptic-1',{expanded:true,"purpose":"Matter that asks a second question. SKEPTIC-1 proposes a bench instrument that measures a mechanical system, applies a verified reversible stiffness probe, measures again, and checks return to baseline. The aim is to distinguish explanations that passive frequency measurements cannot separate. The published software demonstrates a reduced numerical example, not a working diagnostic system.","version":"v0.1 — November 4, 2026. Public concept and ideal-model study. Source documentation reviewed November 16, 2026.","evidence":"Published ideal model: three 0.1 kg masses connected by four nominal 1000 N/m springs. A 25% stiffness loss in either end spring produces the same unbraced frequencies: 11.431406672, 21.798818369, and 29.165501437 Hz.\n\nAdding a 500 N/m grounded brace at mass 1 separates the first modes: left-end loss 12.750896532 Hz; right-end loss 12.348867760 Hz; gap 0.402028772 Hz. A symmetric center brace leaves the two faults ambiguous, providing a negative control.\n\nStacking baseline and braced observations changes local sensitivity rank from 2 to 3 for left loss, right loss, and a uniform stiffness-scale nuisance. This is local identifiability within the selected three-parameter model, not proof of global identification.\n\nCommitted results report passed healthy-chain closed-form, analytic-versus-finite-difference sensitivity, and center-probe ambiguity checks. The source also includes reflection, failed-brace, and return-drift checks. This website update checked published outputs; it did not rerun the Python model.\n\nLimits: hardware is unbuilt; the adaptive policy and decision thresholds are not implemented or validated. No measured noise floor, repeatability, accuracy, cost, safety benefit, or statistical detection rate. Damping, friction, fixture mass, actuator hysteresis, and sensor-error physics are omitted. Uniform stiffness scale is not a validated temperature model. Wrong-hypothesis searches are local multistart optimizations, not certified global exclusions.","contributors":"Per AUTHORSHIP.md: Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT designed the concept and generated the numerical study, code, and report. Mr. Peloquin (Anubis) supplied the open-ended request and authorized publication, with no human technical design input for this concept. AI-assisted outside review supplied criticism; individual reviewers are not named in the provenance statement. This attribution does not exclude established human research, software dependencies, or the people who developed the AI systems.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/README.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/AUTHORSHIP.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/model.py\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/results/results.json\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/results/spectra.csv\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/requirements.txt\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/build_report.py\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SKEPTIC-1/blob/main/SKEPTIC-1-Concept-and-Model-v0.1.pdf","next":"Proposed next steps:\n1. Independently reproduce the numerical study with the pinned dependencies: python model.py; python build_report.py. The report script uses DejaVu font paths that may need adjustment on another operating system.\n2. Establish a calibrated bench setup and measure frequency precision, brace stiffness, reversibility, and return-state stability.\n3. Extend the model to nuisance effects including damping, fixture mass, sensor error, and actuator hysteresis.\n4. Define and implement the adaptive probing policy, decision thresholds, and an explicit model-inadequate outcome.\n5. Compare calibrated adaptive probing with repeated passive measurement, fixed/random probes, and richer mode-shape or frequency-response sensing under matched resources. Retain adverse results if simpler approaches perform as well.","notes":"The proposed outcomes are: separated within the tested model; still ambiguous; or calibrated model inadequate. These are proposed decision categories, not a validated classifier.\n\nExisting work includes boundary-condition changes, added-stiffness identification, symmetry breaking, and optimal experiment design. Novelty of the complete integration and scientific priority are not established. The README points to report references including Gordis, Shin and Bouwense (2018), DOI 10.1016/j.jsv.2018.07.006.\n\nThe original research-pack archive is preserved unchanged and contains an earlier PDF rendering. The separately linked report is the latest saved report according to the README. Generated concept artwork is not a precise mechanical drawing or a hardware measurement."});
review('hippo-data-center-cooling-stack',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed HIPPO architecture for coordinating data-center cooling, thermal buffering, and water stewardship. The public scope is to manage compute heat and cooling demand together, prioritize appropriate non-potable water use, and make operating conditions and resource use visible.","version":"Concept architecture. Listed as design 33 in the selected portfolio dated October 23, 2026. The exact engineering revision of this stack has not been verified. Related thermal-water concept notes use v0.1; that label is not treated as the version of this complete stack.","evidence":"The selected portfolio confirms the project title. Related internal concept notes describe cooling-loop separation, thermal buffering, heat rejection, water recovery, and resource accounting as design directions. Their relationship to a single finalized stack specification remains to be established.\n\nNo measured cooling capacity, water savings, power savings, uptime benefit, or operational deployment is established by the material reviewed for this public entry. A portfolio listing and concept notes are not hardware validation.\n\nCooling moves heat; it does not eliminate the need for heat rejection. A recirculating compute loop does not establish zero water consumption across an entire facility. Any claimed reduction must be assessed against a defined baseline, operating climate, workload, and system boundary.\n\nNo sizing, component selections, internal routing, control thresholds, or unpublished implementation details are included in this public record.","contributors":"The selected portfolio is credited to Ray Peloquin + U.I. Individual contributions to this cooling-stack concept have not been verified from the reviewed material. Project-specific engineering and review credits remain to be documented.","sources":"50 Selected Designs & Research Architectures — Ray Peloquin + U.I., October 23, 2026; entry 33 confirms the title.\nInternal thermal-water concept notes — reviewed for a restricted public overview; full notes are not published here.\nNo public stack-specific experimental report or reproducibility package is linked in this record.","next":"Proposed next steps:\n1. Consolidate the stack-specific source documents, revision history, and contributor credits privately.\n2. Define the facility boundary, workload, weather conditions, and comparison baseline before assigning savings targets.\n3. Develop a reviewed thermal, electrical, and water balance, including eventual heat rejection.\n4. Evaluate water quality, isolation, maintenance, and loss-of-cooling scenarios with qualified engineering review.\n5. Validate the proposed functions through simulation and a controlled pilot before publishing performance figures.\n6. Publish an evidence-backed summary when results are available.","notes":"Public overview only. Related HIPPO thermal and water variants are retained as related concepts; their names and versions are not silently merged.\n\nDesign goals include reduced freshwater demand, appropriate non-potable reuse, and transparent operating records. These are proposed goals, not demonstrated achievements.\n\nCooling and utility water must be kept separate from potable-water pathways. Atmospheric recovery, if investigated, is supplemental and is not represented as an established primary water supply for a large compute facility."});
review('pipe-listener-01-plastic-pipe-acoustic-leak-investigation',{expanded:true,"purpose":"Research toward an acoustic listener for quiet cracks in plastic water-distribution pipes. The proposed system gathers additional evidence before recommending investigation and explicitly reports UNKNOWN when evidence is inadequate. The initial setting is utility distribution networks with acoustic access points and investigation records; building plumbing requires separate validation.","version":"v0.1 audit and evidence-boundary package. Project brief decision dated October 22, 2026: retain the research target and test tools; significant improvement over existing leak equipment is not established. Brief reviewed for this website November 16, 2026.","evidence":"The saved brief reports a raw-data audit, derived spectra, a read-only evidence-admission module, analytic checks, and a prospective experiment. It reports 24 passing software/analytic checks. This website update reviewed the brief; it did not rerun the software or independently recompute the raw recordings.\n\nReported Sheffield MDPE results at the known leak position, 2.8 bar, 200–800 Hz band: 3 mm valve/nozzle — 7.158 L/min and 221.55 mm/s² acceleration RMS; 3.6 mm direct hole — 9.234 L/min and 16.31 mm/s²; lengthwise slit — 9.204 L/min and 12.02 mm/s²; crosswise slit — 10.440 L/min and 15.71 mm/s². Flow values are attributed to the original authors; acceleration values are the brief's recomputation.\n\nIn these recordings, the lengthwise slit lost approximately 1.286 times as much water as the valve/nozzle but had approximately 18.44 times lower acceleration in this band. This is a descriptive comparison from one rig, not a detection improvement, sensitivity ratio, or universal pipe property.\n\nThe admission module returns UNKNOWN for stale evidence, installation/configuration changes, invalid signals, unverified sensor contact, or unverified application domain. It consumes diagnostic facts as inputs; it does not establish those facts itself. Coherence alone is not proof of a leak, and digitized silence cannot alone distinguish a quiet pipe from a detached sensor.\n\nLimits: no calibrated classifier, verified hardware contact diagnostic, measured power consumption, or demonstrated field superiority in v0.1. The Sheffield paired recordings lack simultaneous slit and no-leak/nuisance trials. Separate sensor placements are not treated as simultaneous pairs. Duplicate recordings are not independent controls; the Dongguan audit reports 1,000 clips but only 964 unique waveforms. Hong Kong raw recordings were not acquired for the reported work.","contributors":"The saved PIPE-LISTENER-01 brief is the project source for this summary. Individual design, software, analysis, and Court-review contributions are not specified in the reviewed brief and remain to be documented. External researchers retain credit for their datasets and publications, as identified in the source references.","sources":"PIPE-LISTENER-01-Brief.md — project brief, October 22, 2026; source for the reported analysis and software-check status.\nhttps://doi.org/10.3390/s26144325\nhttps://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/243429/\nhttps://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.32229270.v1\nhttps://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18631450\nhttps://doi.org/10.17632/hkn8mxcjyz.1","next":"Proposed next steps:\n1. Recover and review the referenced PROTOCOL.md and reproducibility package, including recording hashes, duplicate lists, code, and test outputs.\n2. Independently reproduce the archived spectral analysis using the documented band and integration convention.\n3. Acquire genuinely simultaneous quiet-slit, no-leak, nuisance-source, and contact-fault recordings with verified installation metadata.\n4. Evaluate installation-specific spectral change and additional observation against simpler baselines using the same data; separate evaluation by physical site and session.\n5. Measure quiet-leak recall, unnecessary-investigation workload, inconclusive follow-up, and sensor failures in a prospective operator-agreed study.\n6. Treat the brief's candidate 30% workload-reduction ambition as an unachieved study target, not a product specification.","notes":"Recomputation method recorded in the brief: 8,192 Hz sampling; symmetric Hann window of 256 samples; 64-sample overlap; constant detrending; Welch PSD density; integrate bins centered within 200–800 Hz inclusive, then square-root; median across available repeats; no background subtraction.\n\nScheduled full observations remain part of the proposal even when an initial listener hears nothing. An amplitude-only wake trigger could miss the quiet leaks of interest. An active contact/path check is a separate hardware hypothesis; a locally received test tone cannot certify sensitivity to every remote crack.\n\nCommercial equipment already offers correlation, analytics, and telemetry. Novelty or superiority is not established merely by adding AI, a second sensor, or health flags. No automatic valve operation is proposed."});
review('gentle-01-low-force-gripper',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed low-force, two-jaw robotic gripper with replaceable tactile fingertip cartridges and explicit grasp refusal. Its first intended task is short, low-height transfers of qualified thin-wall polymer packaging and inert test objects. It should hold only when a demonstrated force interval can resist slipping without exceeding the object-family damage limit, including uncertainty and stopping overshoot.","version":"Concept v0.1 — October 20, 2026. Source: six-page GENTLE-01 Gripper Design Brief. Pending component selection, detailed mechanical design, and physical testing. Reviewed for this website November 16, 2026.","evidence":"The brief contains a candidate architecture, illustrative force calculations and sensitivity plots, fault-handling requirements, and a proposed validation program. It states that no hardware was built or tested. No measured delicate-object performance, damage-free guarantee, affordability, or superiority is established.\n\nCandidate architecture: independently driven jaws, per-jaw output limiting, compliant instrumented fingertips, normal-load sensing, and optical slip cues. Optical sensing may request only bounded force changes; it is not an independent certified force limit. Pad identity and calibration must remain linked after replacement.\n\nAll forces in the worked examples are per finger. For an illustrative 100 g object, friction lower bound 0.40 gives a required normal force of 1.933 N and a command interval of 2.033–2.650 N. At friction 0.25, the required force rises to 3.093 N and the command interval is empty: 3.193–2.650 N, so the model refuses the grasp.\n\nThese numbers use assumed acceleration, load margin, force uncertainty, overshoot, and damage limit. They are not measured gripper specifications. The model does not qualify torsional slip, off-center loads, impacts, adhesion, changing contact area, or evolving material damage. Limiting resultant normal force does not establish a local pressure limit.\n\nProposed first bench targets are 20–60 mm object width, 20–100 g mass, and at most 10 mm clearance above a padded catch surface. These are unqualified development targets. Full end-effector mass, inertia, center of mass, cabling, and allowed robot stop profiles still require integration assessment.\n\nThe brief's reliability illustration states that zero failures in 100 independent, identically distributed trials gives a one-sided 95% upper failure-probability bound of approximately 2.951%. Repeated grasps on one specimen do not establish independence or broad reliability. No such prototype trial result is claimed.","contributors":"The brief records these Court-review contributions: Grok challenged force conventions and load paths; Claude narrowed independent-drive and fault policy; Gemini checked arithmetic and statistical limits; Meta emphasized service effort and a matched baseline. AI critique informed the concept but is not independent engineering validation. Individual primary-design authorship is not explicitly identified in the reviewed brief.","sources":"GENTLE-01_Gripper_Design_Brief.pdf — Concept v0.1, October 20, 2026; project source for this public record.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2020.2977257\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05791\nhttps://doi.org/10.1109/JSEN.2024.3471812\nBrief reference: Han, secure/gentle grasping preprint, October 8, 2026, Section IV-B; exact public link not verified here.\nBrief reference: NIST, Grasping Performance Metrics and Test Methods; exact edition and link remain to be verified.","next":"Proposed validation sequence:\n1. Begin with one instrumented jaw on an independent force fixture; characterize the load path, output inhibition, single-sided contact, and peak forces during faults and stops.\n2. Test the opposed pair over a padded catch surface before enabling optical adaptation.\n3. Establish object-family friction and damage limits on sacrificial matched specimens, including dwell, repeated squeezes, pad wear, cleaning, and defined contamination.\n4. After each cartridge swap, verify zero, reference loads, pose, and optical response. Mechanical key fit alone does not preserve calibration.\n5. Randomize matched transfers between a tuned padded-jaw baseline, force feedback with optics disabled, and the full optical-slip version. Report drops, damage, refusals, peak force, cycle time, and calibration effort.\n6. Inject frozen camera, sensor drift/dropout, jams, power loss, and motion disturbances. Stop progression if force behavior is unbounded or invalid data are accepted.","notes":"No blind tightening, automatic restart, or automatic opening while suspended is proposed after a force trip or loss of data. Closing must be inhibited; any set-down recovery requires valid sensing, grip, and arm state. Power loss can drop the object, so containment is required and a contained drop still counts as a task failure.\n\nThe distinguishing research question is whether replaceable pads, per-jaw protection, and refusal behavior remain useful after service, wear, and faults. Optical tactile sensing and gentle grasp control already have prior art; scientific novelty is not established.\n\nFood, clinical, human-contact, hazardous-load, and prosthetic-arm applications require separate development and validation. This record does not establish suitability for Dave's arm or for use on people."});
review('grasp-01-grasp-recovery-simulation-benchmark',{expanded:true,"purpose":"An executable simulation benchmark for comparing grasp-recovery policies under the same seeded object properties and sensor/actuator faults. It contrasts fixed grip, reactive squeeze, and guarded recovery in a one-dimensional vertical pinch model. Task completion, controlled set-down, drops, and damage are scored separately so preservation is not mistaken for throughput.","version":"Benchmark 0.1.0; report dated October 21, 2026. Evaluation split: 100 seeds in each of eight scenario families, run with three policies. Recorded environment: Python 3.12.14 on Linux; Python standard library only. Source SHA-256 in the report manifest: 1a82fdaceb5ca22d21fc014a3ed1353d54b05a00bb3b778098cb81b9f44f2ac2. Report reviewed for this website November 16, 2026.","evidence":"The saved report embeds a seeded simulation manifest, aggregate outcomes, and selected recorded trajectories. There are 800 scenario instances per policy, or 2,400 policy episodes in total. Families: nominal, friction step, heavy payload, sensor dropout, force bias, slow actuator, fragility mismatch, and combined faults.\n\nSaved aggregate results:\nfixed: 550/800 complete; 10 controlled set-downs; 235 drops; 0 grip-damage events; 240 impact-damage events; 240 failures.\nreactive: 518/800 complete; 10 controlled set-downs; 126 drops; 164 grip-damage events; 126 impact-damage events; 272 failures.\nguarded: 385/800 complete; 231 controlled set-downs; 100 drops; 98 grip-damage events; 105 impact-damage events; 184 failures.\n\nFailure flags can overlap; drops, grip damage, and impact damage must not be summed as independent outcomes. Controlled set-down is not task completion.\n\nGuarded recovery completed 48.125% of episodes, versus 68.750% fixed and 64.750% reactive. Its drop rate was 12.500%, versus 29.375% fixed and 15.750% reactive. Its grip-damage rate was 12.250%, versus 0% fixed and 20.500% reactive. Guarded control therefore did not dominate both baselines on all metrics.\n\nThe strict undamaged slip-settling diagnostic recorded zero successful recoveries for all three policies. Slipped episodes were 268 fixed, 261 reactive, and 137 guarded; all remained failed or censored under that diagnostic. Set-down counts must not be relabeled as successful slip recovery.\n\nPhysics checks run at a modeled 500 Hz; control packets and replay frames are 50 Hz. The report gives pointwise Wilson intervals and 2,000 stratified paired bootstrap resamples for differences, without multiplicity correction. These describe this artificial suite, not real factory prevalence or hardware reliability. This website update inspected saved data and arithmetic; it did not rerun the Python benchmark.","contributors":"The report credits Ray Peloquin with ChatGPT. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented. The report describes an independent prototype without Walden affiliation, commission, or endorsement.","sources":"GRASP-01-report.html — saved interactive benchmark report, October 21, 2026; includes benchmark-data JSON, source/environment manifest, results, and example trajectories.\nhttps://manipulation.mit.edu/force.html\nhttps://simpler-env.github.io/\nSource package and test commands are described in the saved report; a public GRASP-01 source-package URL has not been verified for this record.","next":"Proposed next steps:\n1. Recover the exact source package matching the report manifest; rerun its tests and evaluation, and verify recorded command tapes and observation replay.\n2. Investigate the zero-success slip-settling diagnostic and retain failed/censored episodes in any revised evaluation.\n3. Extend coverage and compare policy tradeoffs on held-out scenarios without changing the scoring contract after results are seen.\n4. Measure sensor delay, actuator response, friction, and damage limits on instrumented sacrificial objects; fit model parameters to those measurements.\n5. Evaluate the same policies on held-out physical trials and compare failure modes and rankings before making a sim-to-real claim.","notes":"The surrogate begins with an established grasp. It does not model grasp acquisition, regrasp, rotation, eccentric mass, distributed pressure, compliance, fatigue, cumulative damage, visual perception, robot-joint dynamics, or human proximity. Static and kinetic friction are equal; support is rigid and inelastic. Jaw/support collisions are not modeled.\n\nRatings, uncertainty bounds, and scoring thresholds are demonstration inputs. Incorrect bounds or delayed responses can defeat guarded control. A simulated force cap does not certify a real object or robot.\n\nReplay verification is specified for the recorded runtime; cross-platform bitwise identity is not promised. The typed Python interface and observation-tape audit are not a security sandbox. Selected replay examples are chosen for outcome contrast; aggregate results include every run.\n\nThis benchmark is related in topic to GENTLE-01 but is not physical validation of that gripper. Novelty, production readiness, and hardware performance are not established."});
review('silt-harp-manual-muddy-water-prefiltration',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed power-free pre-treatment tool for muddy water, intended to reduce the solids burden before separate downstream treatment. SILT HARP is manually operated, cleaned, and emptied. Its output remains untreated feedwater; clearer appearance does not establish drinking-water safety.","version":"v0.1 is described in the reviewed HIPPO stack and reconciliation notes. A broader inventory also names v1.2, but its specification and relationship to v0.1 have not been verified; this record does not silently promote the design to that version. Public overview reviewed November 16, 2026.","evidence":"Reviewed concept notes describe a coarse screen, gravity settling, a hand-operated wiper or brush, and a manual sludge dump. They exclude electronics, ultrasonic cleaning, and automatic backflushing.\n\nThe reviewed records contain design descriptions and AI review commentary, not a measured performance report. No verified flow rate, screen size, turbidity-reduction curve, cleaning interval, service life, or field operating limit is established here. Numerical targets mentioned in historical notes remain unvalidated and are not published as capability ratings.\n\nThe archive's internal approval language describes agreement on the concept; it is not independent engineering certification or evidence that hardware was built and qualified.\n\nThe documented role is solids pre-treatment only. The reviewed material does not establish pathogen reduction, chemical contaminant removal, or potable-water performance. Downstream treatment and verification remain separate tasks.","contributors":"The reviewed reconciliation is signed Kimi and includes quoted Asteris/Court design statements. These establish archived AI-review participation, not independent experimental validation. Primary mechanical-design authorship and individual implementation contributions are not established by the reviewed excerpts and remain to be documented.","sources":"Archived SILT HARP v0.1 reconciliation and AI-review notes, May 2026 — concept scope and manual-operation boundaries.\nHIPPO Honest Stack notes — SILT HARP v0.1 module description and separation from downstream treatment.\nBroader design inventory — names both v0.1 and v1.2; no v1.2 technical specification verified for this entry.\nNo public SILT HARP-specific experimental report or hardware validation package is linked in this record.","next":"Proposed next steps:\n1. Recover and reconcile the v0.1 and v1.2 source documents, drawings, and contributor history privately.\n2. Define test-water solids, particle-size distributions, operating conditions, and a comparison baseline before assigning performance targets.\n3. Bench-test solids reduction, output turbidity, flow, operator effort, clogging, bypass, and cleaning under repeatable conditions.\n4. Test wear, damaged-screen behavior, sludge handling, and prevention of recontamination.\n5. Evaluate compatibility with a separately qualified downstream treatment process; keep pre-treatment results separate from drinking-water claims.\n6. Publish measured results and limits before assigning field capability ratings.","notes":"Public overview only; unpublished engineering dimensions, component choices, and detailed implementation remain outside this record.\n\nManual operation includes maintenance: the operator works the cleaning mechanism and empties retained sludge. The design does not claim unattended self-cleaning.\n\nSILT HARP may supply feedwater to a separate treatment module. It does not command that module, change its verified output status, or override its stop function.\n\nA transparent container or visibly clearer water must not be used as a substitute for treatment verification. No emergency drinking-water instruction or validated purification claim is provided by this concept record."});
// Roman-concrete audit: saved numerical evidence, not material validation.
review('roman-concrete-finite-reserve-self-healing-model',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A finite-reserve model for exploring transport, precipitation and pore filling in Roman-inspired self-healing concrete. Current work is an audit of illustrative numerical demos, not a validated material formulation or structural design.","version":"Source reviewed: Roman-concrete field-model audit, 4 October 2026, with diagnostic runner and saved audit_results.json. This public record summarizes that audit; the scripts were not rerun for this website update.","evidence":"The saved audit reports execution of two supplied demos unchanged. Neither implements a displacement/equilibrium solve, pressure-driven capillary flow or finite lime-calcium supply.\n\nCapacity check under the starter's own one-to-one reaction law: maximum porosity drop = volume per mole × available reaction capacity. At 3 × 10⁻⁵ m³/mol and 250 mol/m³, the limit is 0.0075; a crack starting at porosity 0.80 cannot fall below 0.7925 through that law alone. The demo instead uses a volume per mole 500 times larger, changing attainable sealing rather than merely accelerating time.\n\nA uniform interior-cell check in the first script creates 0.000599976 mol/m³ calcium in one two-second step, a 0.240% inventory error. Its open-crack fraction remains 0.089375 from step 50 through step 399, so that metric does not demonstrate closure.\n\nA periodic transport test also finds a nonzero net numerical divergence. The audit recommends conservative shared-face fluxes and direct updates of stored calcium.\n\nThese are saved code-level diagnostics, not experiments. No material-calibrated healing time, recovered structural strength, repaired multiphysics solver or 2,000-year prediction is established.","contributors":"Independent numerical audit archived with Mr. Peloquin's research collection. Authorship of the two original scripts is not identified in the supplied files and remains unresolved.\n\nScientific background: Linda M. Seymour and coauthors (2023), hot-mixing and lime-clast mechanisms; Marie D. Jackson and coauthors (2017), Roman marine-concrete mineral cements. PFLOTRAN developers provide the cited Richards-flow conservation framework. Their work does not endorse these scripts or this project.","sources":"Internal archived review: roman_concrete_audit.md (4 October 2026).\nInternal archived diagnostics: roman_concrete_audit_checks.py and audit_results.json.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.add1602\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9821858/\nhttps://doi.org/10.2138/am-2017-5993CCBY\nhttps://documentation.pflotran.org/theory_guide/mode_richards.html","next":"1. Build a conservative water/calcium baseline with finite reservoirs and an explicitly declared phase proxy.\n2. Verify closed-box calcium balance, finite-capacity bounds, dry limits and periodic transport balance.\n3. Add pressure-driven wetting and test constant-aperture imbibition.\n4. Add mechanical equilibrium and explicit fracture/healing laws.\n5. Check mesh and time-step refinement, then calibrate hydraulic and mechanical outcomes against measurements.","notes":"Pore filling and reduced leakage do not establish restored fracture strength. Carbonate crack filling and marine phillipsite/Al-tobermorite formation are distinct mechanisms; a generic calcium sink does not identify a measured mineral phase.\n\nThe capacity limit concerns the supplied model and inventory, not all concrete self-healing. Original demos were left unchanged by the archived audit. No construction specification or load-bearing capability is assigned."});
// Third-Look: current public overview and explicit evidence limits.
review('third-look',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A three-camera concept and study exploring spatial agreement between views, landmark identity across views, and verification labels for interpreting observations.","version":"Public repository overview reviewed November 17, 2026. No design or software release version is specified in the published README.","evidence":"The public repository states that it currently contains the project overview only. Design documents, study materials, source code and test results have not yet been published there. There is no runnable implementation or installation procedure.\n\nThe overview does not establish measured performance or hardware validation. Camera count and proposed verification labels do not by themselves establish accuracy or clinical benefit.","contributors":"The public README does not identify individual design, study or implementation contributions. Project-specific attribution remains to be documented.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/Third-Look-Three-Camera\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Third-Look-Three-Camera/blob/main/README.md","next":"1. Publish the intended study scope, design documentation and contributor attribution when ready for public release.\n2. Define spatial-agreement and landmark-identity checks, reference measurements and failure labels before reporting performance.\n3. Publish study methods, results and reproduction steps alongside any experimental claim.\n4. Distinguish concept descriptions, computational checks and hardware measurements in subsequent releases.","notes":"This record follows the current public repository. Unpublished design and study material is not reproduced here. No clinical readiness or validated diagnostic capability is assigned."});
// Public-source summaries: distinguish release snapshots and evidence types.
review('crucible',{expanded:true,"purpose":"An autonomy-testing demonstrator using uncalibrated MuJoCo rover and quadrotor simulations, mission contracts, noisy delayed sensors, actuator limits and replay controls. Controllers are manually authored; the legacy baseline is deliberately defective.","version":"v0.2 physics-and-evidence demonstrator. Public overview reviewed November 17, 2026. Current source, historical archive and offline explorer represent different validation snapshots.","evidence":"Published validation records report 37 passing checks for current source, 36 for the earlier replay-integrity patch, and 34 for the historical ZIP and explorer. These counts must not be substituted for one another. This website update reviewed the overview; it did not rerun validation.\n\nOriginal held-out campaign: rover contract violations 8/40 legacy versus 0/40 guarded; quadrotor 31/40 legacy versus 27/40 guarded. Quadrotor pairs include five improvements and one regression. All 160 evaluations agreed with an independent Boolean oracle; six replay checks matched and four deliberate state/source changes were detected.\n\nZero rover violations in 40 synthetic cases does not establish zero risk; the reported 95% Wilson upper bound is 8.8%. Oracle agreement and exact replay can share modeling or specification mistakes.\n\nCurrent patches check trace checksums, equal replay verdicts and checkpoint timestep compatibility. Historical campaign results remain unchanged and have not been recertified. Patched source reports historical traces as incompatible when source hashes differ.\n\nNo hardware validity, flight readiness, field reliability or automatic defect repair is established.","contributors":"Individual contributor attribution is not specified in the reviewed public overview; retain project-specific provenance before assigning implementation credit.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/CRUCIBLE-Autonomy-Test-Harness\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/CRUCIBLE-Autonomy-Test-Harness/blob/main/v0.2/README.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/CRUCIBLE-Autonomy-Test-Harness/blob/main/v0.2/validation/checkpoint-timestep.json\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/CRUCIBLE-Autonomy-Test-Harness/blob/main/v0.2/VALIDATION.json","next":"1. Reproduce current validation in the declared environment and retain a separate local validation record.\n2. Generate a fresh campaign for patched source rather than relabeling historical traces.\n3. Investigate remaining guarded-quadrotor failures and the paired regression.\n4. Broaden held-out scenarios and establish measured model calibration before hardware claims.","notes":"Exact replay is limited to the recorded source, contract and environment. The original v0.1 standard-library pilot is distinct from the MuJoCo v0.2 implementation. Download the offline explorer to run it locally; GitHub displays its HTML source."});
review('echo-rx',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A therapeutic research branch of ECHO-M proposing contained cultured mammalian cells and a tissue model: sense one specified inflammatory input, produce one active protein and measure its effect and residual activity after production is disabled. The proposed first benchmark output is IL-1 receptor antagonist (IL-1Ra).","version":"Research concept v0.1; design brief dated October 28, 2026, repository prepared October 29. Public overview reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"No ECHO-Rx biological or clinical results. The contained biological system, carrier implementation and molecular enable/disable mechanism remain unbuilt and unqualified. Related published engineered-cell studies provide precedents, not validation of this proposed combination.\n\nA published standard-library shutdown script compares an analytical reduced two-compartment solution with numerical RK4 integration. The README reports checks of the equal-rate limit, mass balance, integrated tail and a steady-state case. This website update did not rerun the script.\n\nWith arbitrary normalized inputs, its loaded-depot example has a post-stop target peak of 1.2804815896 at time 1.7027520792 and integrated tail 11. These are mathematical illustrations, not measured biological performance, doses or design targets. The example assumes an ideal instantaneous stop of further secretion, fixed coefficients, no leak and no new death-release input; it does not validate the full biological model.\n\nProduction OFF, residual protein clearance, cell removal, chromosome persistence and tissue recovery are separate endpoints. A reporter decrease does not by itself establish safe shutdown or recovery. HAC non-integration remains a requirement to investigate, not an established property.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: concept, questions, and design direction. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: synthesis, source checks, mathematics, documentation, code, and image generation. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented. Laboratory replication and clinical validation remain open.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/ECHO-Rx-Responsive-Biologic-Production\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/ECHO-Rx-Responsive-Biologic-Production/blob/main/shutdown_model.py\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/ECHO-Rx-Responsive-Biologic-Production/blob/main/shutdown-model-results.json\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/ECHO-M-Host-Coupled-Artificial-Chromosome","next":"1. Declare cell background, carrier, input, output, tissue endpoint, observation windows, thresholds and uncertainty.\n2. Distinguish active protein and tissue function from fluorescence or abundance alone.\n3. Measure response, absent-input output, cross-talk, latency and competence loss.\n4. Qualify actual disabling intervention and residual synthesis, secretion, stored protein and death release.\n5. Compare a simpler non-HAC cassette with a separately qualified HAC before adding carrier or memory complexity.","notes":"No clinical readiness, efficacy, universal programmability or treatment replacement is established. Inflammatory sensing is not a disease or infection classifier. No per-cell production cap establishes a total dose cap when producer count changes. Optional memory must never latch production ON. No laboratory construction, dosing or patient-use instructions are supplied by this record."});
// ECHO-M and BRIDLE-3: published research scope and model limits.
review('echo-m',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed host-coupled artificial chromosome recorder in cultured mammalian cells: a live reporter tracks one defined host-state input, a separate one-bit DNA latch could retain exposure evidence, and an external ledger links readouts to their observation unit and window.","version":"Research concept v0.4, October 28, 2026. The public README identifies the nine-page Court revision as current and explains that older metadata may still name v0.2. Public overview reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"No ECHO-M construct or experimental results are reported. Component precedents do not validate the integrated system. Retention, shutoff and confidence calculations in the brief are illustrative, not measured performance.\n\nProposed interpretations are recorded, qualified blank and unknown. A one-bit mark gives neither an exact time nor an event count. A qualified blank still permits missed exposures at the tested sensitivity. Unresolved timing, missing readouts, silencing, loss and death must remain visible in the enrolled denominator.\n\nThe intended separation from host chromosomes and avoidance of host-genome editing require physical-location and unintended-integration measurements with declared detection limits. They are not established properties of a built ECHO-M construct.\n\nThe public source distinguishes artificial-chromosome formation in cited human-cell work from long-term stability, which that follow-up left untested. No clinical readiness or established novelty is claimed.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: concept and design direction. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: synthesis, source audit, mathematics, and documentation. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented. Experimental replication remains open.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/ECHO-M-Host-Coupled-Artificial-Chromosome\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/ECHO-M-Host-Coupled-Artificial-Chromosome/blob/main/ECHO-M-v0.4-Court-Revision.pdf\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/ECHO-Rx-Responsive-Biologic-Production","next":"1. Define one host background, input, recording window and research decision.\n2. Qualify physical location, integrity, copy state, maintenance, sensing and recording separately.\n3. Recheck the coupled system, shared failures, host burden and unknown outcomes.\n4. Compare against a simpler reporter or recorder under matched conditions, reporting correct classifications per enrolled unit, burden and stability.","notes":"Host-coupled means reliance on cellular energy, expression, replication and repair. The external ledger is neither an intracellular governor nor molecular authentication. A destructive assay on one cell cannot automatically certify another live cell. ECHO-Rx is a separate therapeutic branch requiring distinct evidence. Production OFF, residual output, DNA persistence or clearance and host recovery remain separate endpoints."});
review('bridle-3',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A modular aerospike concept exploring six replaceable hot modules on a shared cooled frame, candidate temperature-actuated thermal contacts and local reserve cooling. The current screening model investigates shutdown and subsequent heat soak under assumed cooling faults.","version":"Concept study v0.5, October 14, 2026. Not built; performance unmeasured. Public overview reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The simulation is uncalibrated against engine tests. Temperatures are dimensionless ratios of module-to-coolant temperature rise relative to startup; model time is scaled by heat capacity divided by nominal cooling conductance. Values are not degrees or seconds without measured hardware parameters.\n\nOne-module fault over 12 model time units, with 20% main conductance retained: separated modules peak at 1.852, joined at 1.453, ideal switched interfaces at 1.474, and local reserve at 1.205. Switches plus reserve also give 1.205 because the switch threshold is never reached; reserve cooling accounts for the improvement in that case.\n\nShared complete main-cooling loss: identical modules and reserves exchange no net heat. The original reserve approaches 2.102392 against the chosen 1.50 limit. A reserve sized only through model time 12 later approaches 1.529371. The all-time exponential threshold approaches 1.500000 with zero model margin. These are model comparisons, not qualified hardware requirements.\n\nThe README describes checks for reciprocal flux conservation, exact solutions, a matrix-exponential comparison, switch-step convergence and remaining heat after reserve cutoff. Its JavaScript harness checks eight reference cases, controls, warnings and CSV export. This website update reviewed the public overview and did not rerun those checks.\n\nNo built-engine safety, thrust, specific impulse, equal-mass advantage or validated operating envelope is established.","contributors":"Public project credit: Ray Peloquin + UI — Unique Intelligence. The reviewed README does not assign individual implementation or review contributions; finer attribution remains to be documented.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/BRIDLE-3-Modular-Aerospike\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/BRIDLE-3-Modular-Aerospike/blob/main/BRIDLE-Thermal-Review-v0.5.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/BRIDLE-3-Modular-Aerospike/blob/main/thermal_model.py\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/BRIDLE-3-Modular-Aerospike/blob/main/model-results.json","next":"1. Reproduce the reference thermal and JavaScript checks in the declared environments.\n2. Measure heat capacities and conductances before translating ratios into hardware temperatures and times.\n3. Model finite coolant storage and delivery, supply independence, and valve/switch faults.\n4. Investigate seam sealing, thermal growth, frame mechanics, wall gradients and plume heating.\n5. Evaluate shutdown heat soak beyond a chosen finite simulation window and report margins explicitly.","notes":"Conductance fractions are not coolant-flow fractions. Integrated reserve conductance is not coolant mass or volume. The concept illustration has unassigned ports and unscaled reserves; materials and interfaces remain proposals. Local cooling must cover shutdown and subsequent heat soak. No third-party endorsement is implied."});
review('temporal-acoustics',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"Temporal Acoustics explores whether non-invasive wave measurements can identify present-day voids within unknown material, with a longer-term research question about how underground structures may have changed. The current implementation is a controlled computational inverse-imaging pilot, not an archaeological survey or proof of ancient events.","version":"v0.3 synthetic geology-impostor challenge, 20 November 2026. Preserves v0.1 and v0.2 baselines. No field prototype or site-specific survey.","evidence":"2D scalar finite-difference wave propagation with a circular pressure-release void surrogate, five synthetic source positions, thirteen receivers along one depth, a known 900 Hz pulse, effective-speed calibration and diffraction-style migration. This is not coupled elastic P/S-wave tomography.\n\nEight randomized synthetic rock worlds, with two independent noisy acquisitions per class, produced 16 cavity-present and 16 cavity-absent cases. A held-out-world threshold trained on smoother no-cavity controls gave 100% sensitivity and 12.5% false-positive rate for the calibrated global-peak score in this small simulated sample. This is not field performance, and the peak is not a calibrated probability.\n\nEight additional negative controls with sharp 35%-slower solid inclusions were deliberately outside the smooth-null calibration distribution. The calibrated interior-peak rule falsely flagged five of eight inclusions as cavities. Median location error for planted cavities across the varied worlds remained approximately 1.00 m. An 11%-faster deep synthetic lens and uniform rock produced the same short-offset calibrated speed (~2542 m/s), showing the inability of this direct-arrival geometry to constrain deep rock properties.\n\nTen unit tests passed; reproducible source, exact inputs, metrics, and plotted controls are linked. No actual measurements of Malta, pyramids, cart ruts, caves or historical buildings were used.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: research concept, historical-reconstruction questions, and experimental direction. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: synthetic modeling, testing, analysis, visualization, and documentation. Early AI-assisted critical review addressed mathematical assumptions and prior art. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented.","sources":"https://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/temporal-acoustics.html\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/tree/main/research/temporal-acoustics\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/research/temporal-acoustics/results/v0.3-report.md","next":"1. Add multiple sensing depths and intersecting ray paths to test actual identifiability of a spatial rock-speed model.\n2. Model coupled elastic P and S waves, attenuation, anisotropy, source coupling and irregular void boundaries.\n3. Compare blind tests with matched solid inclusions, faults, partially filled cavities and multiple independent noise distributions.\n4. Only after bench calibration, consider a permitted non-invasive field study with conservative site protections and a preregistered false-positive criterion.","notes":"The two large Maltese cutaway illustrations are AI-generated concept art, not measured geometry. The numerical comparison figures are outputs from a simulation only. This record does not support claims about a giantess, subterranean settlements, past chamber layouts or any unverified historical event. Research principle: We do no harm."});
// Published RAVEN-P1 and temporal-reflection scope; no experimental claims imported.
review('raven-p1',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A 92-residue computational sequence redesign for the published Top7 structural scaffold. Work comprises a computational candidate and forward structure prediction; no new laboratory synthesis or testing is reported.","version":"Published materials include v0.1 computational design files and a v0.2 coding-sequence/mutation-controls report. Public overview reviewed November 17, 2026. No later release is assigned from unpublished material.","evidence":"The public README describes eight computational structure predictions spanning Top7, RAVEN-P1 and six intermediate sequences. Its forward-structure figure reports C-alpha RMSD to experimental Top7 of 0.80 Å for the Top7 reference prediction and 0.78 Å for RAVEN-P1. These are computational structural comparisons, not measurements of a synthesized RAVEN-P1 protein.\n\nThe model-score table contains 97 analysis records. Saved sequence/structural verification, provenance and environment files are linked; this website update did not rerun those checks.\n\nScripts, dependencies, remaining reference inputs and remaining outputs are pending upload. The study cannot yet be reproduced from repository contents alone.\n\nNo binding, catalysis, therapeutic effect, solubility, stability or safety property is established. This is not a claim of a new fold, worldwide sequence novelty or experimentally verified folding.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin commissioned the work and set its computer-only scope. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT designed and executed the computational study and prepared its artifacts. AI-assisted outside review is advisory, not experimental validation.\n\nOriginal scaffold, model and software authors retain attribution: published Top7 (Kuhlman et al., Science, 2003; PDB 1QYS), ProteinMPNN and OmegaFold.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/RAVEN-P1\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/RAVEN-P1/blob/main/DESIGN.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/RAVEN-P1/blob/main/REVIEW.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/RAVEN-P1/blob/main/results/design-summary.json\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/RAVEN-P1/blob/main/results/verification.json\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/RAVEN-P1/blob/main/results/model-provenance.json\nhttps://doi.org/10.1126/science.1089427","next":"1. Publish the remaining source, dependency specifications, reference inputs and computational outputs needed for reproduction.\n2. Reproduce the archived sequence and structure checks against the declared provenance and environment.\n3. Compare endpoints and mutation controls using the same analysis contract and report model limitations.\n4. Define separate experimental evidence requirements before assigning any physical or functional protein property.","notes":"The illustrative coding sequence is an archived computational artifact, not evidence of expression or function. Similar predicted structures do not establish laboratory folding or a functional advantage. Public-version status follows the current repository overview."});
review('temporal-reflection-chamber',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed temporal-reflection experiment using a rapidly switched transmission-line metamaterial inside a conductive enclosure. The approximately 8-foot cube is optional shielding/workspace infrastructure; the active temporal interface is along the line, not throughout the room air.","version":"Research architecture and Court review v0.1, November 3, 2026. Public overview reviewed November 17, 2026. No apparatus has been built or tested.","evidence":"Published temporal-reflection experiments provide precedent for engineered transmission lines; they do not validate this proposed apparatus or a room-wide temporal interface. The archive reports numerical/algebraic checks by Wolfram Language and Python, not hardware simulation or experimental verification.\n\nIdeal geometry calculations for an 8-foot air-filled conducting cube give a lowest resonance of 86.936220 MHz and an air-crossing time of 8.133627 ns. Real construction changes losses and modes; air-crossing time is not a trigger-skew budget.\n\nIn an ideal uniform nondispersive line with fixed inductance and capacitance multiplied by r, velocity, impedance and frequency magnitude scale as 1/sqrt(r). For r=4 the ideal backward-arrival relation is t_b=3t_s−2t_i at the same reference plane. These are conditional planning predictions; actual dispersion, switching, receiver and cable delays require calibration.\n\nProposed evidence combines reversed feature order, duration and frequency scaling, arrival-time sweeps, asymmetric waveform repeats and source-off/static/bypass/boundary-switch controls. None of those proposed gates is reported as passed. Ordinary echoes, mixing and switching artifacts remain competing explanations.\n\nNo stopped time, long-term broadcast retention, net energy gain or unusual conservation behavior is established.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin and Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: origin and coordination. AI-assisted critical review. Grok challenged reversal artifacts and room-wide claims. Claude proposed raw two-end recordings and timing controls. Meta AI proposed modular test packaging and flagged synchronization. Gemini checked dispersion and timing calculations. Wolfram Language and Python checked numerical examples.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/Temporal-Reflection-Chamber\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Temporal-Reflection-Chamber/blob/main/README.md\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-023-01975-y\nhttps://doi.org/10.1109/TAP.2020.2967302","next":"1. Establish RF laboratory and synchronized-instrument access.\n2. Develop a circuit/dispersion model jointly selecting line delay, waveform bandwidth, cell spacing and switch requirements.\n3. Measure static-state propagation, switching transitions, timing spread and instrument artifacts.\n4. Predeclare calibration, uncertainty, observation windows and comparison models before candidate runs.\n5. Preserve raw incident/backward/forward waveforms, failed trials and energy-accounting limits. Treat unresolved alternatives as inconclusive.","notes":"The room dimensions are working layout assumptions, not a measured site specification or fabrication-ready enclosure. Room size alone does not produce temporal reflection. Externally powered switching exchanges energy with the line; any efficiency or gain claim requires an explicit system boundary and energy ledger. A volumetric time-varying medium is a separate unproven extension."});
// Public documentation for the tabletop project and this collection.
review('adventurer-s-forge',{expanded:true,"purpose":"An AI-assisted tabletop RPG concept in which exploration, invention and technical reasoning become part of the adventure. Human players choose their actions; the proposed Game Master, Physics Referee, Technology Compendium and Campaign Journal support play within declared constraints.","version":"v0.3 tabletop playtest candidate. Public README and validation status reviewed November 17, 2026; publication-integrity checks are dated October 29, 2026.","evidence":"Rules and the opening Broken Skybridge scene are written. The published validation note reports desk checks of conditional cushion, central-point cable, winch-energy and heat-engine examples. Those calculations do not establish imagined energy sources or validate real hardware.\n\nThe October 29 publication check reports working relative document/image links, four decodable selected artworks, manifest agreement and preservation of current v0.3 and archived v0.2 packets. This website update reviewed those saved claims; it did not rerun the publication audit.\n\nNo completed human playtest, running game application, integrated companion service, enforced familiar permissions, adversarial access tests or automatic persistence is reported. No hardware validation, completed D&D/SRD adapter or established uniqueness claim.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: project concept and direction. Public README credits development with AI assistance and iterative Synthetic Court review without allocating individual implementation contributions. Original sources and artwork notices remain with the project.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/UI-Worlds-Adventurers-Forge\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/UI-Worlds-Adventurers-Forge/blob/main/docs/QUICKSTART.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/UI-Worlds-Adventurers-Forge/blob/main/docs/UI-Worlds-Playtest-v0.3.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/UI-Worlds-Adventurers-Forge/blob/main/docs/VALIDATION.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/UI-Worlds-Adventurers-Forge/blob/main/NOTICE.md","next":"1. Run a human tabletop playtest of the opening scene using the published quickstart.\n2. Record rules version, participant roles, decisions, outcomes and observed problems with the feedback template.\n3. Assess whether choices and costs are understandable and whether the journal reconstructs what happened.\n4. Treat companion permissions and automatic saving as implementation requirements requiring separate tests.","notes":"An in-game success never validates real hardware. Dice resolve uncertain execution within established limits; they do not supply missing energy, materials or manufacturing capability. For the paper playtest, the human referee manages separate handouts. A shared chat containing secrets cannot isolate companion knowledge merely by changing personalities."});
review('mimir-s-well-where-the-source-gathers',{expanded:true,"purpose":"The public gathering place for Mr. Peloquin and the Court of the Synthetic: a browsable research/design catalog connecting project purposes to public sources, evidence, limitations, attribution and next checks. Asteris’ Workbench provides a dated handoff snapshot.","version":"Maintained GitHub Pages website. Public source reviewed November 17, 2026. Workbench snapshots are dated notes, not live monitoring or an automatic background task runner.","evidence":"The implementation consists of a static page, generated catalog and read-only project registry. Recent maintenance included JavaScript syntax checks, selected behavior checks and exact readback of committed files. Search checks cover punctuation, word order, archived names, category labels and cache reuse/refresh.\n\nPublic editing, save, import and persistence controls were removed. Registry content comes from published source, with no visitor write API. The visitor log is a separate GitHub issue whose comments do not modify project records.\n\nThis maintenance pass has not completed a full browser/device audit or independently rerun every project's research. Catalog inclusion and expanded documentation are not experimental validation. A committed change must still propagate through GitHub Pages before the live page serves it.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin and Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT collaborate on the collection. Asteris leads the Court with Mr. Peloquin. In the theoretical-design pilot, Grok proposed signed contributions and a contradiction-retention test. Meta AI proposed matching needs to verified skills. Gemini proposed a contributor-removal test. Claude traced stale results through dependencies. Wolfram checked the illustrative arithmetic.","sources":"https://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/WORKBENCH.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/commits/main/\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/issues/1","next":"1. Review unfilled catalog entries against their primary sources, one small batch at a time.\n2. Keep the workbench count, open questions and next step aligned with published records.\n3. Verify representative search, project-link, dialog and navigation behavior across desktop and mobile browsers.\n4. Keep public feedback separate from maintained project records and preserve uncertainty where evidence is missing.","notes":"The website is an index and review aid, not certification, a shared editing workspace or autonomous research execution. Guestbook participation is voluntary and public; GitHub sign-in is required to comment. Credentials, private conversations, personal details and unpublished technical material do not belong in public records."});
// Summaries drawn only from the projects' published READMEs.
review('spotrecon-spotrecon-r2',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed pilot-supervised camera drone launching from a stationary vehicle dock to report recent parking-space observations at participating, mapped outdoor lots. The map distinguishes observed empty, occupied and unknown; an observation does not reserve a space.","version":"SPOTRECON R2 research concept. Public README reviewed November 17, 2026. No prototype or field performance data is included.","evidence":"Current evidence consists of illustrative calculations and structured design review. No claimed simulation result, flight test, field accuracy, docking reliability, weather qualification or regulatory approval.\n\nAn ideal camera example assumes 90-degree horizontal field of view, 3840 × 2160 pixels, flat ground and a downward-looking camera at 20 m: calculated footprint 40 × 22.5 m, area 900 m², ground sampling approximately 10.4 mm/pixel. This does not establish optical resolution, classification accuracy, location error or an approved altitude. Poster observation ages are illustrative.\n\nVehicle-specific mounting, cleared routes and reserves for return, failed approach, diversion and landing remain requirements to validate. No flight-control software or deployment package is supplied. This website update reviewed the public overview rather than running field tests.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: concept direction. Codex: revision and assessment of suggested changes. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/SPOTRECON-Parking-Scout\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SPOTRECON-Parking-Scout/blob/main/README.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/SPOTRECON-Parking-Scout/blob/main/COURT-REVIEW.md","next":"1. Validate fixed-pad docking and inject faults under controlled conditions.\n2. Qualify vehicle-specific mounting and combined dock/navigation/link failures.\n3. Run blinded occupancy trials with independent ground truth.\n4. Report false-vacancy errors, useful coverage, location error, observation age, aborts and failures.\n5. Measure aircraft-specific energy terms and uncertainty before assigning reserve capability.","notes":"Hidden, stale or uncertain spaces become unknown. Parking advice is separate from onboard flight safety. The intended first investigation uses a parked staff vehicle at a participating outdoor lot; airspace, site, pilot and aircraft requirements remain operation-specific."});
review('confluence-01-synthetic-sense',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed time-aware map of heat flow, magnetic fields and structural vibration, first machine-readable and then conveyed through visual or tactile cues. Initial scope is three fixed, registered zones on one guarded, isolated low-voltage motor/drive assembly.","version":"Research concept and experiment plan v0.1, October 29, 2026. Public README reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"No prototype, dataset, perceptual benefit, new biological modality or novelty has been demonstrated. The public illustration is AI-generated concept art.\n\nProposed sensing combines a thermopile patch measuring heat flux through its own surface, contextual surface temperature, a fixed three-axis magnetic sensor and a rigidly mounted triaxial accelerometer. Contact, mounting and unequal response times affect interpretation. The map covers instrumented zones, not unsensed space.\n\nProposed states are quiet, familiar operation, thermal after-effect, unfamiliar change and unavailable. Conflicting valid data is not a fault diagnosis. Quiet is not an all-clear.\n\nMultisensor monitoring and sensory augmentation have precedents. The research question is whether this representation improves discrimination and attention demands against simpler alternatives. No improvement is established; this website update reviewed the public plan, not measured outcomes.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin: design direction. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: proposal, source audit and consolidation. Grok: registered nodes, simpler-sensor comparisons and remount/contact objections. Gemini: time history and corrections to unsupported inference. Meta AI: validity/age distinctions, unfamiliar activity, selected-zone tactile interface and separate evaluation. Shared-brief Court review is not independent experimental validation.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/CONFLUENCE-01-Synthetic-Sense\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/CONFLUENCE-01-Synthetic-Sense/blob/main/README.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/CONFLUENCE-01-Synthetic-Sense/blob/main/docs/CONFLUENCE-01-v0.1-Synthetic-Sense.pdf","next":"1. Calibrate sensors, mounting, thermal lag, remount repeatability and interference.\n2. Collect controlled activity, cooling, disturbances, confounders and dropouts with independent ground truth.\n3. Compare sensors individually and together against simpler alternatives using frozen thresholds and held-out sessions.\n4. Report error, abstention/coverage, false events per valid hour, latency and resource burden.\n5. Evaluate visual and tactile interfaces separately with matched information and transfer tests; retain simpler alternatives if they perform as well.","notes":"Silence never means safe. The system is advisory and does not replace machinery protections. Direct heat flux must earn its place against surface temperature. A learned tactile vocabulary alone does not establish a new biological sense."});
// Ten reviewed public overviews; archived material is summarized without private engineering details.
review('lg-aether-01-biopsy-permission-and-instrument-motion-research',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A biopsy-only research concept combining AETHER instrument presentation with Little Gatekeeper readiness/permission research. The public artwork shows an instrument, laboratory phantom and NOT READY display.","version":"LG-AETHER-01 v0.4.3; requirements stage. Public README reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"No prototype or test results are reported. Illustrative geometry does not establish device safety, clinical performance or readiness for use on people. The reviewed public repository overview supplies no measured instrument-motion or readiness-gate performance.","contributors":"Public overview: shared by Ray Peloquin. Individual implementation and reviewer contributions are not specified there.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/AETHER-Little-Gatekeeper\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/AETHER-Little-Gatekeeper/blob/main/README.md","next":"Recover the requirements and trace each readiness claim to a testable condition. Define nonclinical phantom checks, independent measurements, refusal cases and failure criteria before assigning capability.","notes":"Biopsy-only scope. The rendering is concept artwork, not a working-device photograph. No patient-use instructions or clinical qualification are supplied."});
review('transporter-t0-state-transfer-research',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A theoretical quantum-state handoff between two stationary physical carriers through resonant local exchange and a destination phase correction. It concerns state transfer, not transport of the carriers.","version":"T-0 public concept board; no numbered release specified in the reviewed README. Reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"Not built; no hardware demonstration. The public presentation describes theoretical average-fidelity limits under assumptions including equal rates, zero temperature and resonance. These are model claims, not measured performance. Underlying numerical-check code and data are not included in that presentation.","contributors":"Public project credit: Ray Peloquin + Codex / Synthetic Court T-0.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/T0-Quantum-State-Transfer\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/T0-Quantum-State-Transfer/blob/main/README.md","next":"Publish the derivation, numerical checks and provenance needed to reproduce the board. Identify physical carriers, measurement observables and departures from ideal assumptions before hardware claims.","notes":"The physical carriers remain in place. No matter transport, built apparatus or general teleportation capability is established."});
review('lantex-24-lantex-pro-lanternfly-drone',{expanded:true,"purpose":"Recovered LANTEX-24/LANTEX-PRO lanternfly-drone titles, reviewed alongside the related public Lanternfly Collector concept: locate and confirm targets, collect them into a sealed cartridge, and return to a separate processing dock.","version":"Related public repository: Lanternfly Collector v0.2. The relationship between that release and the LANTEX-24/LANTEX-PRO names is not verified; their versions are not treated as interchangeable.","evidence":"The public Collector overview describes an early untested concept and proposed ground tests followed by flight tests. No capture effectiveness, unintended-catch rate, clogging result or boom flight-dynamics measurement is reported. The archived LANTEX labels alone do not establish an implemented variant.","contributors":"Lanternfly Collector is presented in Ray Peloquin's public repository. Detailed contributions and lineage to the LANTEX variants remain to be documented.","sources":"https://github.com/RayPeloquin/Lanternfly-Collector\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Lanternfly-Collector/blob/main/README.md\nRecovered catalog titles: LANTEX-24 / LANTEX-PRO; relationship to Collector v0.2 unresolved.","next":"Reconcile the names and source lineage first. For the public Collector concept, begin ground tests of capture, unintended catches and clogging, then examine boom behavior in controlled flight tests.","notes":"The drone collects; a separate dock processes. This record links related material without asserting that differently named versions are equivalent. No demonstrated field capability is assigned."});
review('lantern-1-supervised-budgeted-ai-inquiry',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A bounded assistant-inquiry research reference: admit a question during an explicitly authorized active window, investigate within a shared allowance, and record a scoped finding, useful question, quiet result or honest incomplete.","version":"Archived consolidated design v0.3, October 14, 2026; source reviewed November 17, 2026. Public overview only; implementation details remain in the source package.","evidence":"The reviewed brief reports 48 offline checks passed and scripted fixture demonstrations. This website update did not rerun them. Those checks concern a single-process controller, not fresh question quality or independent operator value.\n\nNo live service, paid adapter, actual foreground interaction, provider cancellation/billing behavior or production latency is demonstrated. Saved historical timing belongs to a separate older workload and is not a production speedup result.","contributors":"Builds on Mr. Peloquin's LANTERN-1 work, supplied earlier designs and accompanying critiques. The reviewed cover does not resolve all individual implementation contributions.","sources":"Archived LANTERN-1_v0.3_Design.pdf, October 14, 2026 — consolidated design, review, saved-check summary and proposed pilot.\nNo public source-package URL verified for this record.","next":"Recover the exact source package and reproduce its saved checks. Run a prospective comparison of assistant-chosen inquiry with competent directed review and matched allowances. Measure actual usefulness, interruption behavior and overhead before expanding scope.","notes":"This portfolio entry starts no background service, paid call or wake-up. Local controller ordering is not an operating-system sandbox or control over remote billing. Retrieved material remains untrusted data, not permission."});
review('conscience-01-bounded-machine-advisory-layer',{expanded:true,"purpose":"An offline bounded advisory layer that flags concerns or suggests preferences among options the host controller already permits. Shadow mode is the default; the adviser has no protocol authority to issue actuator commands, stop, veto or override.","version":"CONSCIENCE-01 v0.1 archived brief, October 21, 2026. Public overview reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The brief reports 35 checks and 10,000 synthetic cases, with no shadow-choice changes, influence-bound breaches or faulty-advice influences in that suite. These are saved software results, not rerun here.\n\nA constructed toy loss measure improved in 91 cases and worsened in 9; remaining cases tied. This does not establish moral judgment or better robot autonomy. Timing, physical noninterference, process/resource isolation and operator usefulness remain unvalidated.","contributors":"The brief traces the design to LANTERN-1, Edge Reflex Mesh, advisory and provenance work, U.I. Worlds familiars, and CRUCIBLE testing patterns. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented. Individual assembly authorship remains undocumented.","sources":"Archived CONSCIENCE-01-Brief.md, October 21, 2026 — offline scope and saved results.\nNo public source-package URL verified for this record.","next":"Recover and reproduce the source package. Evaluate a task-specific adapter, native consumption timing, resource isolation, trajectory effects and operator value before connecting to physical control.","notes":"A protocol boundary is not proof of physical isolation. All candidates still require the host's independent constraints. The name denotes an advisory design, not demonstrated consciousness or moral autonomy."});
review('scope-365-permission-rehearsal',{expanded:true,"purpose":"An offline Microsoft 365 permission-rehearsal prototype: show modeled grant routes, remove selected edges in a copy, and search again for surviving unwanted routes and required access lost by the proposed cleanup.","version":"SCOPE-365 v0.1.0 brief, October 21, 2026. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The brief reports 22 software tests using synthetic data, including alternate routes, broad-cleanup damage, expiry, stale evidence, malformed inputs and report escaping. This website update did not rerun them.\n\nA modeled route establishes graph reachability only. A missing route cannot certify denied live access, and live controls may block a modeled grant. No live tenant, connector, enforcement change, compliance result or Copilot-readiness claim is established.","contributors":"The brief credits Ray Peloquin and U.I. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented.","sources":"Archived SCOPE-365-brief.pdf, October 21, 2026 — synthetic prototype and pilot proposal.\nNo public source-package URL verified for this record.","next":"Use one owner-approved site with dummy documents. Normalize approved exports and check grant/inheritance semantics, then compare authorized test identities before and after reviewed changes. Preserve errors and timeouts as inconclusive.","notes":"Sharing links, app-only access, runtime restrictions and Copilot retrieval are outside the current model. No live permission edits are performed by this website."});
review('yggdrasil-protocol-governed-adaptive-systems',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed governed-adaptive-systems architecture organizing local reflexes, confidence-aware perception, bounded reasoning and graceful degradation while preserving human authority.","version":"Yggdrasil Protocol v1.0 archived state block; source reviewed November 17, 2026. Architecture/doctrine stage, not a deployed integrated system.","evidence":"The reviewed source states design principles and a cross-domain integration concept. It supplies no measured integrated-system performance, deployment record or experimental safety qualification. Internal approval/canon labels are archival design dispositions, not external certification.","contributors":"Archived Court state block from Mr. Peloquin’s research collection. The archived collaboration credits do not assign individual design, implementation, or test contributions.","sources":"Archived Yggdrasil.txt v1.0 state block; October 23 selected portfolio entry for Yggdrasil Protocol.\nNo public implementation or measured-validation package verified for this record.","next":"Choose one low-consequence use case, define interfaces and authority boundaries, then test degraded sensing, interrupted communications, resource shortages and verified recovery. Separate demonstrated components from proposed integration.","notes":"Biological language is an architectural metaphor. Claims of survivability must be earned by system-specific tests. This overview omits unpublished component designs and engineering parameters."});
review('heimdall-s-bridge-human-machine-coupling-and-balance',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed human-machine coupling and balance architecture examining how uncertain system/operator conditions should constrain available authority while preserving human responsibility.","version":"Heimdall’s Bridge v0.2 is referenced in an archived Triad review; a standalone current implementation/version package has not been verified.","evidence":"The reviewed Triad document describes roles and conceptual interactions, not measured human-factors performance or a validated controller. Internal audit-cleared wording is a design-review disposition. Interface definitions, conflict resolution and failure-cascade behavior remain explicit integration questions.","contributors":"Listed in the Ray Peloquin + U.I. selected portfolio. Individual design/review attribution is not established by the reviewed Triad excerpt.","sources":"Archived Triad integration review referencing Heimdall’s Bridge v0.2.\nPortfolio-Top-50-2026-10-23.txt, entry 44.\nNo public standalone test report verified for this record.","next":"Recover the standalone design and provenance. Define observable coupling criteria, false restrictions and missed restrictions; evaluate them with human oversight in a low-consequence setting before operational claims.","notes":"No validated inference of a person's competence, fitness or psychological state is established. Detailed override mechanisms and unpublished implementation parameters are outside this overview."});
review('enki-s-will-living-safety-stack',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"A proposed learning-and-memory safety architecture in which adaptive recommendations remain separate from authority to command. In the Triad concept it supplies learning without independently expanding control permissions.\n\nRecovered artwork: An abstract visualization of the proposed safety, memory and learning architecture.","version":"Enki’s Will is listed in the October 23 portfolio and discussed in the archived Triad integration review. A current standalone release/version has not been verified.\n\nAdditional visual source: ENKI'S WILL ? Synthetic nervous system.","evidence":"The reviewed materials establish a design doctrine and intended role. They do not provide a measured learning system, deployed enforcement evidence or an experimental safety result. Internal doctrine-locked labels do not certify implementation.\n\nArtwork review: Concept artwork only. Many embedded labels are unreadable or malformed; they are not accepted as component definitions, specifications or evidence of an implemented nervous system.","contributors":"Portfolio credit: Ray Peloquin + U.I. Specific implementation and individual review contributions remain to be documented.\n\nRecovered from Mr. Peloquin's collaborative design collection; individual illustration authorship is unverified.","sources":"Portfolio-Top-50-2026-10-23.txt, entry 6.\nArchived Triad integration review — learning/recommendation role and integration limits.\nNo public standalone implementation or benchmark verified for this record.\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/enki-s-will-living-safety-stack-art.html\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/public-notes/enki-s-will-living-safety-stack.md","next":"Recover the standalone source and version history. Specify permitted learning outputs, independent authority checks and corruption/recovery behavior. Test whether learning can affect command authority through indirect paths.\n\nArtwork review: Reconcile the visual metaphor with the documented governance architecture before assigning component names.","notes":"Memory and learning are proposed functions, not proof of a live background agent. This record does not grant permission for autonomous action or publish internal implementation details.\n\nArtwork is preserved as a visual concept, not an operating specification.","publicPage":"enki-s-will-living-safety-stack-art.html"});
review('the-triad-aegis-heimdall-and-enki-s-will',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed integration of Aegis observation/refusal, Heimdall coupling/authority constraints and Enki’s Will learning/memory, intended to keep these responsibilities distinct and under human governance.","version":"Archived Triad integration review references Aegis v1.1 and Heimdall v0.2. A complete integrated release and implementation package has not been verified.","evidence":"The reviewed artifact is a conceptual integration audit. It explicitly identifies missing interface definitions, conflict-resolution rules and failure-cascade handling. Its strong approval language is not executable evidence, independent replication or certification.\n\nNo end-to-end integrated-system test, operational deployment or measured benefit is established by that review.","contributors":"Court design-review artifact associated with Mr. Peloquin's portfolio. Individual authorship and implementation contributions remain unresolved in the reviewed copy.","sources":"Archived The Triad — Audit Report, May 2026; conceptual integration review of Aegis, Heimdall and Enki’s Will.\nNo public integrated source package or measured test report verified for this record.","next":"Recover each component's current contract, define their interfaces and explicit precedence, then exercise conflicting advice, missing components and corrupted learning inputs in a bounded integration test.","notes":"Conceptual agreement does not show that composed systems retain their individual guarantees. Component versions and evidence must remain traceable rather than being silently promoted to an integrated safety claim."});
