review('hypha-mind-go-cube-update-authorization-testbed',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A toy registry and authorization-interface study comparing full reconstruction with authenticated local update witnesses.","version":"Integration Gate v0.2; reporting clarification v0.2.1, October 14, 2026. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The reviewed report preserves the failed submitted v0.1 run separately from the corrected local v0.2 pilot. The frozen pilot reports 456 scenario rows, 18 commit controls and 640 agreeing benchmark pairs. Shared controller and validation code limits independence. The witness route does not immediately detect arbitrary backing-store corruption. Hash-work ratios are not elapsed-time ratios; instrumented timing is not a production guarantee. No learning-quality, complete GO-CUBE-governor, action-safety or fundamental-time result is established. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Joint prototype and Court-results artifact; independent implementation and reproduction remain open. No Court consensus is reported.","sources":"Archived Hypha-Mind-SMT-R1-Gate-v0.2-Court-Results.md, including reporting clarification v0.2.1.","next":"Recover the frozen source/evidence packet, reproduce decisions and roots, retain the failed v0.1 evidence, and commission a separate implementation before generalizing.","notes":"A locally frozen computational pilot is not external preregistration or formal verification. Authentication, durability and deployment claims require separate evidence. Related but distinct design concepts now have their own public pages: GO-CUBE Liberty Field Governor (go-cube.html) and the parent Court Governance Stack (court-governance-stack.html). These concept diagrams do not inherit the update-testbed pilot\u0027s computational evidence. The update testbed does not validate the wider governor."});
review('sr-margin-01-spacecraft-power-and-thermal-audit',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A read-only energy-ledger audit adapter and synthetic power/thermal test bench, retained after a more complex warning predictor failed to improve the declared endpoint.","version":"Archived outcome brief, October 21, 2026. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The brief reports 72 tuning and 144 evaluation episodes. All four monitors gave the specified timely warning in 60 of 72 true-crossing cases and missed it in 12. All 12 bias-only cases produced false margin warnings; 36 healthy cases had no alerts. Fifteen analytic/boundary checks passed. Numerical energy closure verifies bookkeeping, not spacecraft fidelity. Shared-loop counterfactuals show that dumped electrical energy remains heat to reject; they are not validated spacecraft operating modes. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Ray Peloquin / U.I. — Unique Intelligence. The record documents AI-assisted critical review. Individual reviewer contributions remain undocumented. Independent execution remains open.","sources":"Archived SR-MARGIN-01-Brief.md, October 21, 2026.\nhttps://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20240002420\nhttps://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20210020739\nhttps://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20220002047","next":"Retain the simpler audit/fault fixtures and negative predictor result. Seek an owner-confirmed interface problem and representative simulator/test-stand traces before developing a flight component.","notes":"No actuator or reactor connection. The prescribed heater is not a reactor model; sensitivity samples do not establish a guaranteed safety envelope."});
review('signature-selective-receiver-detection-and-error-bounds',{expanded:true,"purpose":"An analytic and synthetic reference connecting declared waveform/interference uncertainty to a receiver statistic, conditional error bound and received-energy requirement.","version":"Derivation and synthetic verification note, October 17, 2026. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The archived note reports 27 synthetic checks. In its constructed example, robust receiver predicted worst conditional BER is 0.20905%, compared with 0.21300% simulated. Each receiver is tested at its own least favorable admitted disturbance, not a common field environment. The result depends on the declared geometry and noise model. Gaussian tail guarantees do not follow from covariance alone. No prototype, circuit reconstruction or measured supply-efficiency advantage is established. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Prepared for Ray Peloquin. The note distinguishes its calculations from established robust-filtering literature and Nikfal, Zhang and Caloz's phaser work; it does not reconstruct their undisclosed receiver.","sources":"Archived signature-receiver-derivation.md, October 17, 2026.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1109/LMWC.2014.2348792\nhttps://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1983.1056734\nhttps://www.jmlr.org/papers/v3/lanckriet02a.html","next":"Reproduce the synthetic packet, then measure actual signatures, timing variation, interference, noise, dynamic range and implemented response. Run blinded link trials and account for total supply energy per correctly delivered payload bit.","notes":"Keep wrong, correct and inconclusive outcomes together. A taller filtered peak does not itself create information or establish system energy savings."});
review('field-medic-guided-hand-support',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A nonclinical wearable guided-hand concept using backpack/hip support, articulated arm supports and gloves around the wearer's own hands. Intended assistance remains task-bounded and human-controlled.","version":"Consolidated v0.3 concept, October 14, 2026. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The reviewed six-page brief supplies proposed requirements and illustrative arithmetic, not new device measurements. Current development boundary is inert fixtures and mannequins. Passive support, cues/rehearsal, bounded powered guidance and fault response have distinct requirements. Logical revocation does not prove zero residual motion or force. No clinical capability, broad novice suitability or autonomous trauma-care benefit is established. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin's FIELD MEDIC research collection; individual implementation and review attribution is not resolved by the reviewed brief. Cited haptic research is precedent, not validation of this device.","sources":"Archived Field-Medic-v0-3-Guided-Hands.pdf, October 14, 2026.\nhttps://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2021.716598\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2401.11709","next":"Start with one supported glove and one instrumented inert task. Establish fit, independent metrology, complete fault-response bounds and stop/release behavior before powered wearer trials; assess benefit only after prerequisites pass.","notes":"Human enable is an observed input, not proof of informed consent, mental intent, trajectory authorship or legal liability. No patient-use procedure is provided."});
review('edge-reflex-mesh-local-control-and-safety-reflexes',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed layered physical-AI architecture keeping safety reflexes local, onboard reasoning near the machine, coordination on site and cloud functions advisory.","version":"EDGE REFLEX MESH v0.4 archived state block. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The source describes controller roles, proposed latency budgets, watchdog/heartbeat concepts and fallback behavior. No measured latency distribution, built safety controller, independent timing validation or field safety result is supplied. Labels such as buildable and sub-30 ms describe design intent, not demonstrated performance. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Archived design associated with Mr. Peloquin's portfolio. Individual implementation and review contributions remain to be documented.","sources":"Archived EDGE REFLEX MESH v0.4.txt.\nOctober 23, 2026 selected portfolio, entry 7.","next":"Select one low-consequence platform, define task-specific safe transitions, and measure sensing-to-actuation behavior under overload, stale intent, link loss, power loss and common-cause faults.","notes":"A watchdog or cloud-advisory label does not by itself establish independence or a safe physical stop. Unpublished component choices and detailed timing budgets remain outside this overview."});
review('truth-layer-confidence-and-uncertainty-overlay',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed confidence/uncertainty overlay for industrial operators, using installed telemetry and provenance to make stale signals, disagreement and unresolved interpretation visible.","version":"TRUTH LAYER v0.1 archived industrial-overlay proposal. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The reviewed material is an architecture and pilot proposal, not measured alarm reduction, faster decisions, compliance or commercial readiness. Its performance percentages and deployment schedule are unvalidated targets. The source mixes advisory framing with protective-action proposals; control authority and acknowledgement/fault behavior require reconciliation before implementation. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Archived Court design proposal in Mr. Peloquin's collection. Specific implementation authorship and independent reviewer attribution remain to be established.","sources":"Archived industrial TRUTH LAYER v0.1 proposal, Pasted text(135).txt.\nOctober 23, 2026 selected portfolio, entry 15.","next":"Narrow to a read-only pilot, define signal-quality and uncertainty semantics, reconcile control boundaries, and compare against existing displays using held-out events and operator workload measures.","notes":"No recommendation is a substitute for qualified plant protection. Claims about timelines, regulatory readiness and industrial-to-medical transfer are not adopted as established results."});
review('quantum-observability-layer-infrastructure-telemetry',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed human-centered visualization of quantum-infrastructure telemetry: calibration freshness, environmental conditions, operational trends and uncertainty.","version":"Quantum Observability Layer v0.1 in archived quantum floor.txt; broader inventory versions are not reconciled. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The reviewed state block supplies visualization requirements and proposed integrations, not measured operator benefit or an integrated deployment. It describes infrastructure observations rather than direct viewing of a wavefunction. Named integration and approval labels do not establish functioning interfaces or certification. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Listed in Ray Peloquin + U.I.'s selected portfolio. Individual implementation and review attribution remains to be documented.","sources":"Archived quantum floor.txt, Quantum Observability Layer v0.1 section.\nOctober 23, 2026 selected portfolio, entry 47.","next":"Identify available telemetry and its measurement limits. Build a bounded replay display and test stale/conflicting/missing data, false precision and operator comprehension against a simpler dashboard.","notes":"The overlay cannot infer an unseen quantum state from display design. Uncertainty and unavailable states must remain explicit."});
review('quantum-floor',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"A proposed classical scheduling/stewardship layer for imperfect quantum infrastructure, coordinating work with calibration quality, environmental conditions and resource fairness.\n\nRecovered artwork describes classical environmental stewardship, workload scheduling and a speculative research roadmap for quantum infrastructure.","version":"Reviewed archive includes Quantum Floor v1.1 and a related v1.0 orchestration proposal. Catalog variants are not silently treated as one validated release. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.\n\nRecovered visual sources: Heimdall's Quantum Floor v1.1; Adaptive Quantum Floor v1.0; Heimdall's Gaze roadmap; provisional family association.","evidence":"The source describes pre-execution, between-shot and calibration-window orchestration. It provides targets and a proposed validation strategy, not completed randomized hardware benchmarks, shot-efficiency improvements or validated timing. Internal fully certified wording is not evidence of independent certification. Proposed subsystem composition has not been demonstrated by the reviewed artifact. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.\n\nNo demonstrated improvement in coherence, fidelity, throughput, or facility reliability. Roadmap dates and numerical targets are aspirational, not predictions or results. Quantum hardware temperature requirements are platform-specific.","contributors":"Archived Court architecture in Mr. Peloquin's collection; per-module implementation and review provenance remains to be established.\n\nRecovered artwork supplied from Mr. Peloquin's collaborative design collection; individual illustration authorship is unverified.","sources":"Archived quantum floor.txt, Quantum Floor v1.1 and related orchestration sections.\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/quantum-floor-artwork.html\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/public-notes/quantum-floor-artwork.md","next":"Reconcile versions and freeze an executable scheduler contract. Compare against a declared baseline on recorded traces before hardware trials; evaluate fairness, calibration impact, delay and failed/aborted work.\n\nCompare scheduling and environmental models against ordinary baselines. Keep HIPPO-linked integrated architecture nonpublic.","notes":"This is classical orchestration, not a quantum computer or a demonstrated coherence improvement. A proposed integration cannot inherit unmeasured guarantees from named components.\n\nSome original artwork remains outside public Git; only bounded summaries are supplied for those sources.","publicPage":"quantum-floor-artwork.html"});
review('cyber-meta-material',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A proposed semantic-coherence and interface-continuity design for synthetic assistants: keep roles, evidence labels and public/private boundaries intelligible across changing contexts.","version":"CMM-1 v0.3.1 state-block draft; archive labels it L3/V1 tabletop-reviewed and explicitly not approved or operational. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"The reviewed artifact is a conceptual state block and tabletop-review disposition. It does not demonstrate a physical material, autonomous continuity service, deployed drift detector or validated security mechanism. Metaphors and maturity labels are not implementation evidence. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.","contributors":"Archived design in Mr. Peloquin's research collection. Individual implementation contributions and independent validation are not established in the reviewed copy.","sources":"Archived Pasted text(335).txt, CMM-1 v0.3.1 state block.","next":"Define observable role/boundary errors and a bounded evaluation corpus. Compare corrections, false alarms and failure cases before making continuity or enforcement claims.","notes":"Public overview only. Private continuity anchors, security responses, tool-authority details and unpublished implementation specifics are omitted. The material metaphor does not imply biological life or physical substance."});
review('court-of-the-synthetic-multi-model-engineering-review',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"A human–AI collaboration method for questioning designs, comparing criticism, checking sources and revising claims. Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT leads in collaboration with Mr. Peloquin.\n\nRecovered artwork: A symbolic depiction of the Court review roles and Forge method.","version":"Current public collection and project-specific review records, reviewed November 17, 2026. No single software release is assigned to the method. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.\n\nAdditional visual source: Mount Olympus Design Model ? Hera variant.","evidence":"Public project documentation records contributions from multiple AI systems and selectively adopted corrections. Shared briefs, common sources and model agreement are not independent replication. The website demonstrates an organized collection, not a fully automated multi-agent platform or universal validation process. This website update reviewed the saved source; it did not rerun the research or perform hardware tests.\n\nArtwork review: The illustrated council is a visual metaphor for the collaboration method, not a depiction of actual people, independent experimental review, institutional endorsement or deployed agents.","contributors":"Mr. Peloquin and Asteris Solenne / ChatGPT: collaboration method and review coordination. Individual credits appear on the records where their work is documented.\n\nRecovered from Mr. Peloquin’s collaborative design collection. Individual illustration authorship is unverified.","sources":"https://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/#court\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/mimirs-well/blob/main/WORKBENCH.md\nhttps://github.com/RayPeloquin/Temporal-Reflection-Chamber/blob/main/README.md\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/court-of-the-synthetic-multi-model-engineering-review-art.html\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/public-notes/court-of-the-synthetic-multi-model-engineering-review.md","next":"Keep review provenance, adopted/rejected objections and evidence levels traceable. Seek independent reproduction where experimental or software claims warrant it, and retain disagreements rather than converting overlap into consensus.\n\nArtwork review: Retain role descriptions and contributions in the existing review records.","notes":"A continuity persona is an interface identity, not a claim of sentience. The Court provides AI-assisted critical review. Certification and experimental evidence require their own documented basis.\n\nArtwork is preserved as a visual concept, not an operating specification.","publicPage":"court-of-the-synthetic-multi-model-engineering-review-art.html"});
