review('authority-at-commit',{expanded:true,"purpose":"A small executable reference showing why permission must still be valid when a protected write occurs, rather than relying on an earlier preflight approval.","version":"Authority at Commit v0.1, October 22, 2026; archived Python fixture and results. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.","evidence":"Rerun during this review: 17 functional checks and 200 controlled real-thread schedules passed; four deliberately broken variants were detected. All 100 revoke-first schedules refused the write with zero protected mutations; all 100 commit-first schedules retained the earlier authorized write. Rerun output exactly matched archived results. Events force the two orders; this is not exhaustive concurrency exploration.\n\nOne process, one in-memory resource, trusted request context, a shared lock, a public test HMAC key and a local clock. No distributed-revocation, crash-durability, real identity-provider, tamper-resistant audit or remote-effects guarantee. Other write paths outside the model remain out of scope.","contributors":"Prepared by Mr. Peloquin with U.I. according to the archive README; the present maintenance pass reran the unchanged fixture.","sources":"Archived Authority-at-Commit-v0.1.zip, README.md, fixture.py and results.json.","next":"Publish a reproducible source package with revision/environment details, then choose a separate distributed or crash-recovery question rather than extending the local result by assertion.","notes":"Revocation does not undo an earlier completed write. HMAC in this fixture detects claim edits; it does not independently authenticate the human, agent or runtime. No OAuth conformance or novel-standard claim."});
review('weaver-s-shuttle',{"expanded":true,"purpose":"A defensive security architecture proposal separating admission evidence, permission checks, availability protection and bounded containment of suspicious traffic.\n\nRecovered artwork: A later illustrated Neith Protocol / LANTERN Gate concept for defensive command admission and accountability, distinct from the previously reviewed v1.4 bench text.","version":"Reviewed bench revision v1.4, codename Neith Protocol / LANTERN Gate. Later versions mentioned elsewhere were not reviewed here. Source reviewed November 17, 2026.\n\nAdditional visual source: THE WEAVER'S SHUTTLE v2.0.","evidence":"The reviewed state block is a corrected architecture and staged validation roadmap, not a measured hardware-path proof, deployed gateway or detection benchmark. A hardware-presence or time-attestation claim still needs an explicit threat model and experiments. Review acceptance does not establish unhackability, reliable detection or safe availability behavior. This website pass reviewed saved documentation; it did not rerun the underlying system or conduct physical/clinical experiments.\n\nArtwork review: The poster's clearance and security claims are not independent verification. Signed assertions are not necessarily true; emergency-access labels do not authorize disabling protective safety systems.","contributors":"Archived Court-corrected design in Mr. Peloquin's collection; project-specific implementation and independent test provenance remain unresolved.\n\nRecovered from Mr. Peloquin's collaborative design collection; individual illustration authorship is unverified.","sources":"Archived Pasted text(194).txt, Weaver’s Shuttle v1.4 bench revision.\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/weaver-s-shuttle-art.html\nhttps://raypeloquin.github.io/mimirs-well/public-notes/weaver-s-shuttle.md","next":"Start with harmless synthetic traffic and a shadow model. Measure false rejection, resource exhaustion, clock/environment faults and emergency access before any inline integration.\n\nArtwork review: Test defensive admission and availability with synthetic data in isolated environments; preserve independently enforced safety protections.","notes":"Defensive scope only; containment stays inside controlled infrastructure. No autonomous retaliation, external probing or deployment is established. Private enforcement and hardware details are omitted.\n\nArtwork is preserved as a visual concept, not an operating specification.","publicPage":"weaver-s-shuttle-art.html"});
